# config/arch/Kconfig.isa-x86_64 — x86_64: baseline level and extensions (n / probe / require)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# x86_64 (port skeleton)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The same three tiers riscv64 uses (crates/core/arch-riscv64/src/cache.rs):
#   1. the compile-time BASELINE (X86_64_LEVEL), the only thing codegen may
#      assume, checked at boot: a CPU below it is refused with a message;
#   2. optional extensions, each a runtime CPUID probe before use with a
#      fallback (crates/core/arch-x86_64/src/features.rs), emitted only
#      inside gated asm unless set to `require`;
#   3. each extension a three-way choice X86_* (n / probe / require, the
#      model above); per-board defaults say what a board has. `require`
#      adds the extension's target feature (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py): the
#      integer ones to the kernel and the user images, the SIMD ones to the
#      user images only (the kernel is soft-float). A level that contains
#      an extension offers only `require` for it, and an extension whose
#      prerequisite is `n` (AVX without XSAVE, AVX2 without AVX, ...) offers
#      only `n`: a contradiction cannot be configured, and a hand-edited
#      .config that has one is refused by crates/core/limits/build.rs.

if ARCH_X86_64

choice
    prompt "x86_64 baseline micro-architecture level"
    default X86_64_LEVEL_V2
    help
      The psABI level codegen assumes for the whole kernel and the user
      images (`-C target-cpu=x86-64[-vN]`, tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py). Anything
      above it is an optional extension (X86_* below). The boot CPU
      checks the level's CPUID bits before anything else and refuses to
      boot below it. Higher levels let the compiler use wider instructions
      everywhere (smaller, faster code) and drop every CPU older than the
      level.

    config X86_64_LEVEL_V1
        bool "x86-64 (v1: SSE2)"
        help
          Every x86_64 CPU. No popcnt/SSE4.2 in compiled code.
    config X86_64_LEVEL_V2
        bool "x86-64-v2 (SSE4.2, POPCNT, CMPXCHG16B)"
        help
          Nehalem (2008) and later, every QEMU `-cpu` model newer than
          qemu64 with `+sse4.2,+popcnt`. The default.
    config X86_64_LEVEL_V3
        bool "x86-64-v3 (AVX, AVX2, BMI1/2, F16C, FMA, LZCNT, MOVBE, XSAVE)"
        help
          Haswell (2013) and later. AVX2 becomes baseline; kernel FP/SIMD
          use still needs the FPU-section discipline (FP_XSAVE_EAGER). The
          soft-float kernel gains only the integer part (+bmi1, +bmi2,
          +lzcnt, +movbe, +xsave); the user images get all of it.
    config X86_64_LEVEL_V4
        bool "x86-64-v4 (+ AVX-512 F, BW, CD, DQ, VL)"
        help
          Skylake-SP / Ice Lake / Zen 4 and later. The kernel's codegen is
          the same as v3 (AVX-512 is SIMD, which the soft-float kernel never
          emits); the user images get AVX-512 everywhere, and every task's
          XSAVE area grows by the 2 KiB ZMM/opmask state. Excludes every
          client Intel CPU from Alder Lake on (AVX-512 fused off).
endchoice

choice
    prompt "SSE4.2 (with SSE3, SSSE3, SSE4.1)"
    default X86_SSE4_2_REQUIRE if !X86_64_LEVEL_V1
    default X86_SSE4_2_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[20] (and [0], [9], [19]). SIMD: the soft-float kernel
      never emits it; require adds +sse4.2 to the user images' codegen.
      probe: exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_SSE4_2 in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap) (no kernel
      path uses it). Cost: none. Part of x86-64-v2: at that level and above only `require` is
      offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_SSE4_2_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on X86_64_LEVEL_V1
    config X86_SSE4_2_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on X86_64_LEVEL_V1
    config X86_SSE4_2_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "POPCNT"
    default X86_POPCNT_REQUIRE if !X86_64_LEVEL_V1
    default X86_POPCNT_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[23]. require adds +popcnt to the kernel and user codegen
      (count_ones() becomes one instruction instead of a ~12-instruction bit
      trick). probe: exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_POPCNT in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap).
      Cost: none. Part of x86-64-v2: at that
      level and above only `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_POPCNT_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on X86_64_LEVEL_V1
    config X86_POPCNT_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on X86_64_LEVEL_V1
    config X86_POPCNT_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "XSAVE (XSAVE/XRSTOR, XCR0)"
    default X86_XSAVE_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V3 || X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_XSAVE_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[26]: the extended FP state save (fpu.rs, FP_XSAVE_EAGER)
      and the XCR0 register every AVX state needs. Fallback: FXSAVE (512 B,
      x87/SSE only), which also means no AVX anywhere. require adds +xsave.
      Cost: the XSAVE area per task (576 B + each enabled component). Part
      of x86-64-v3: at that level and above only `require` is offered (the
      level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_XSAVE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_XSAVE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_XSAVE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AVX (256-bit SIMD, YMM state)"
    default X86_AVX_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V3 || X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_AVX_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[28] and the XCR0 YMM component (CPUID.(0xD,0)). User
      images only (require adds +avx to their codegen); the kernel reaches
      it only inside an FPU section. Cost: +256 B of YMM state per task in
      the XSAVE area. Needs X86_XSAVE. Part of x86-64-v3: at that level and
      above only `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AVX_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_AVX_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2) && !X86_XSAVE_NEVER
    config X86_AVX_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_XSAVE_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AVX2 (256-bit integer SIMD)"
    default X86_AVX2_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V3 || X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_AVX2_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[5] plus the AVX state. Used ONLY inside the Vector
      impl's dot product, behind the probe and the FPU section. Fallback:
      SSE2 (baseline). require adds +avx2 to the user images. Exported to
      ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_AVX2 in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap), set only with the YMM
      state enabled in XCR0. Cost: the YMM
      half of the FP save area (shared with X86_AVX). Needs X86_AVX. Part of
      x86-64-v3: at that level and above only `require` is offered (the
      level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AVX2_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_AVX2_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2) && !X86_AVX_NEVER
    config X86_AVX2_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_AVX_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "BMI1 (ANDN, BEXTR, BLSI, BLSR, TZCNT)"
    default X86_BMI1_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V3 || X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_BMI1_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[3]. Integer: require adds +bmi1 to the kernel and the
      user images (shorter bit-field and lowest-set-bit code). probe:
      exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_BMI1 in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap). Cost: none. Part of x86-64-v3: at that level and above
      only `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_BMI1_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_BMI1_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_BMI1_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "BMI2 (BZHI, PDEP, PEXT, SHLX/SHRX, MULX)"
    default X86_BMI2_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V3 || X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_BMI2_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[8]. Integer: require adds +bmi2 to the kernel and the
      user images (flag-free shifts; no CL-register pressure). probe:
      exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_BMI2 in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap). PDEP/PEXT are microcoded (~250 cycles) on AMD before
      Zen 3. Cost: none. Part of x86-64-v3: at that level and above only
      `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_BMI2_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_BMI2_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_BMI2_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "FMA3 (fused multiply-add)"
    default X86_FMA_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V3 || X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_FMA_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[12] plus the AVX state. User images only (require adds
      +fma). Exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_FMA in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap), set
      only with the YMM state enabled in XCR0. Cost: none beyond X86_AVX's
      state. Needs X86_AVX. Part of
      x86-64-v3: at that level and above only `require` is offered (the
      level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_FMA_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_FMA_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2) && !X86_AVX_NEVER
    config X86_FMA_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_AVX_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "MOVBE (byte-swapping load/store)"
    default X86_MOVBE_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V3 || X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_MOVBE_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[22]. Integer: require adds +movbe to the kernel and the
      user images (big-endian network fields load in one instruction).
      probe: exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_MOVBE in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap). Cost:
      none. Part of x86-64-v3: at that level and
      above only `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_MOVBE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_MOVBE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on (X86_64_LEVEL_V1 || X86_64_LEVEL_V2)
    config X86_MOVBE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AVX-512 Foundation"
    default X86_AVX512F_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_AVX512F_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[16] and the XCR0 opmask/ZMM components (bits 5..7).
      User images only (require adds +avx512f). Cost: +2 KiB of ZMM/opmask
      state per task in the XSAVE area, and frequency drops on some Intel
      parts while 512-bit code runs. Needs X86_AVX2. Part of x86-64-v4: at
      that level and above only `require` is offered (the level compiles it
      in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AVX512F_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    config X86_AVX512F_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4 && !X86_AVX2_NEVER
    config X86_AVX512F_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_AVX2_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AVX-512 Byte and Word"
    default X86_AVX512BW_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_AVX512BW_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[30]. User images only (require adds +avx512bw). Cost:
      none beyond X86_AVX512F. Needs X86_AVX512F. Part of x86-64-v4: at that
      level and above only `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AVX512BW_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    config X86_AVX512BW_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4 && !X86_AVX512F_NEVER
    config X86_AVX512BW_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_AVX512F_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AVX-512 Conflict Detection"
    default X86_AVX512CD_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_AVX512CD_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[28]. User images only (require adds +avx512cd). Cost:
      none beyond X86_AVX512F. Needs X86_AVX512F. Part of x86-64-v4: at that
      level and above only `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AVX512CD_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    config X86_AVX512CD_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4 && !X86_AVX512F_NEVER
    config X86_AVX512CD_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_AVX512F_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AVX-512 Doubleword and Quadword"
    default X86_AVX512DQ_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_AVX512DQ_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[17]. User images only (require adds +avx512dq). Cost:
      none beyond X86_AVX512F. Needs X86_AVX512F. Part of x86-64-v4: at that
      level and above only `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AVX512DQ_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    config X86_AVX512DQ_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4 && !X86_AVX512F_NEVER
    config X86_AVX512DQ_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_AVX512F_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AVX-512 Vector Length (128/256-bit forms)"
    default X86_AVX512VL_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    default X86_AVX512VL_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[31]. User images only (require adds +avx512vl). Cost:
      none beyond X86_AVX512F. Needs X86_AVX512F. Part of x86-64-v4: at that
      level and above only `require` is offered (the level compiles it in).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AVX512VL_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4
    config X86_AVX512VL_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_64_LEVEL_V4 && !X86_AVX512F_NEVER
    config X86_AVX512VL_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_AVX512F_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AES-NI"
    default X86_AES_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[25]. User images only (require adds +aes): link encryption
      and TLS in ring 3 run AES in ~1 cycle/byte instead of ~15. probe:
      exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_AES in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap) (the kernel's
      crypto is portable). Cost: none.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AES_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_AES_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_AES_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "PCLMULQDQ (carry-less multiply)"
    default X86_PCLMULQDQ_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[1]. User images only (require adds +pclmulqdq): GCM's
      GHASH and CRC folding. probe: exported to ring 3 as
      HWCAP_X86_PCLMULQDQ in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap). Cost: none.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_PCLMULQDQ_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_PCLMULQDQ_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_PCLMULQDQ_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "SHA-NI (SHA-1/SHA-256 instructions)"
    default X86_SHA_NI_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[29]: sha256rnds2 and friends. The kernel's own
      secure-boot hash does not use it yet; exported to ring 3 as
      HWCAP_X86_SHA_NI in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap). require adds +sha to the user
      images.
      Fallback: the portable SHA-256 (crates/core/crypto). Cost: none.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_SHA_NI_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_SHA_NI_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_SHA_NI_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "RDRAND (DRBG output)"
    default X86_RDRAND_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[30]. A seed source for the boot entropy pool when RDSEED
      is not in use (DRBG output: mixed, never credited, so RDRAND alone
      never seeds the pool). Exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_RDRAND in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap).
      require adds +rdrand. Fallback: the pool's other sources.
      Cost: one 96-byte draw at boot; each RDRAND is ~100-500 cycles.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_RDRAND_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_RDRAND_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_RDRAND_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "RDSEED (conditioned entropy)"
    default X86_RDSEED_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[18]. The preferred seed source for the boot entropy
      pool (conditioned entropy, not DRBG output; credited 1:1 under
      ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE=credit). Exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_RDSEED in
      the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap). require adds +rdseed. Fallback: RDRAND as
      X86_RDRAND says, then the pool's other sources. Cost: one 96-byte
      draw at boot. n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks.
      require: the boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_RDSEED_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_RDSEED_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_RDSEED_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "ADX (ADCX/ADOX)"
    default X86_ADX_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[19]. Integer: require adds +adx to the kernel and user
      codegen (two carry chains for multi-precision arithmetic: Ed25519,
      RSA). probe: exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_ADX in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap).
      Cost: none.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_ADX_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_ADX_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_ADX_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "FSGSBASE (rd/wrgsbase)"
    default X86_FSGSBASE_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[0]: detected and reported only; NOT used. CR4.FSGSBASE
      is never set, so ring 3's rd/wrfsbase and rd/wrgsbase #UD and
      HWCAP_X86_FSGSBASE reads 0. Wiring it needs the context switch to
      save and restore every user task's FS base and its GS base (waiting
      in IA32_KERNEL_GS_BASE) -- today only a Linux task's FS base is
      switched, by MSR, and no GS base is -- which is more than the enable.
      The kernel's own per-CPU base stays on rdmsr/wrmsr IA32_GS_BASE. Not
      a codegen feature (rustc has no stable +fsgsbase): require only
      refuses a CPU without it.
      n: no difference. probe: no difference (the boot line only). require:
      the boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_FSGSBASE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_FSGSBASE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_FSGSBASE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "PCID (tagged TLB)"
    default X86_PCID_PROBE
    help
      CR4.PCIDE after CPUID.1:ECX[17]. Keeps TLB entries across address-
      space switches (the ASID of Mmu::switch_pt). Fallback: a full flush on
      every CR3 write. Cost: none; saves the TLB refill after each switch.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_PCID_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_PCID_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_PCID_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "INVPCID (targeted TLB invalidation)"
    default X86_INVPCID_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EBX[10]: flush one PCID or every non-global entry with one
      instruction. Fallback: CR4.PGE toggle / CR3 reload. Used only with
      PCID on. With TLB_ASID_SCOPED a shootdown's own CPU names the space's
      PCID (type 0 / 1) instead of marking itself stale. Cost: none.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_INVPCID_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_INVPCID_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_INVPCID_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "INVLPGB (broadcast TLB invalidation)"
    default X86_INVLPGB_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.0x80000008:EBX[3] (AMD Zen 3 and later): INVLPGB invalidates a
      range of pages on every CPU, the issuing one included, and TLBSYNC
      waits for it. The TLB shootdown (`ArchPlatform::tlb_shootdown`,
      `flush_tlb_page_all`) then sends no IPI, takes no request lock and
      marks no CPU stale under TLB_RETAIN. Fallback: the shootdown IPI.
      n: the IPI always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the boot
      refuses a CPU without it. A hypervisor that hides the bit (QEMU TCG)
      runs the IPI path.

    config X86_INVLPGB_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_INVLPGB_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_INVLPGB_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "SMEP (supervisor-mode execution prevention)"
    default X86_SMEP_PROBE
    help
      CR4.SMEP after CPUID.(7,0):EBX[7]. Cost: none at runtime. Off: the
      kernel may execute user pages (an exploit primitive).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_SMEP_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_SMEP_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_SMEP_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "SMAP (supervisor-mode access prevention)"
    default X86_SMAP_PROBE
    help
      CR4.SMAP after CPUID.(7,0):EBX[20]; the user-access window
      (ArchPlatform::user_access) is stac/clac. Cost: two instructions per
      user copy. Fallback: no window (every kernel access to user memory
      allowed), as without the bit.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_SMAP_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_SMAP_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_SMAP_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "UMIP (user-mode instruction prevention)"
    default X86_UMIP_PROBE
    help
      CR4.UMIP after CPUID.(7,0):ECX[2]: SGDT/SIDT/SLDT/SMSW/STR fault in
      ring 3, so user code cannot read kernel table addresses. Cost: none.
      Fallback: the instructions stay legal (a KASLR leak).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_UMIP_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_UMIP_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_UMIP_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "PKU (protection keys for user pages)"
    default X86_PKU_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):ECX[3]: detected and reported only; NOT used. A hook for
      the 16 user page keys in PKRU (XSAVE component 9): no kernel code sets
      CR4.PKE, writes a pkey into a PTE or switches PKRU, there is no
      pkey_mprotect, and HWCAP_X86_PKU reads 0. The plan reserves a pkey slot in the page
      table entry for it. Needs X86_XSAVE.
      n: no difference. probe: no difference (the boot line only). require:
      the boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_PKU_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_PKU_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_XSAVE_NEVER
    config X86_PKU_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_XSAVE_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "LA57 (5-level paging)"
    default X86_LA57_NEVER
    help
      CR4.LA57 after CPUID.(EAX=7,ECX=0):ECX[16]: 57-bit virtual
      addresses (64 PiB of user space instead of 128 TiB) through a PML5
      above the PML4. boot.S decides it before paging is on (CR4.LA57
      cannot change in long mode), and crates/core/arch-x86_64/src/mmu.rs
      reads the depth back from CR4. Cost: one more level on every TLB
      miss (one more memory access per walk, 4 KiB more per address
      space). Only for a machine whose RAM or VA needs exceed 64/128 TiB.
      n: 4-level always (the depth is then a compile-time 4). probe: 5
      levels when the CPU has it. require: the boot refuses a CPU without
      it.

    config X86_LA57_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_LA57_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_LA57_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "CET indirect-branch tracking"
    default X86_CET_IBT_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):EDX[20]: indirect jumps and calls must land on ENDBR64.
      Any policy but n builds the kernel and the user images with
      `-Z cf-protection=branch`, and the boot sets CR4.CET and
      IA32_S_CET.ENDBR_EN on every CPU that has it
      (crates/core/arch-x86_64/src/cpu/cfi.rs): ring 0 is enforced, ring 3
      is not (IA32_U_CET is per process). Exported to ring 3 as
      HWCAP_X86_CET_IBT in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap), set
      once the kernel sets CR4.CET. Cost when used: an ENDBR64 per function
      entry (4 B); the CET state is an XSAVES supervisor component. Needs
      X86_XSAVES.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_CET_IBT_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_CET_IBT_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_XSAVES_NEVER
    config X86_CET_IBT_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_XSAVES_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "CET shadow stack"
    default X86_CET_SHSTK_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(7,0):ECX[7]: a hardware return-address stack (the x86 twin of
      aarch64 PAuth return signing). Not enabled yet. Exported to ring 3 as
      HWCAP_X86_CET_SHSTK in the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap), set only once the kernel
      sets CR4.CET (it does not yet, so the bit reads 0). Cost when
      used: one shadow-stack page set per task and the CET XSAVES state.
      Needs X86_XSAVES.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_CET_SHSTK_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_CET_SHSTK_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_XSAVES_NEVER
    config X86_CET_SHSTK_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_XSAVES_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "XSAVEOPT (modified-only FP save)"
    default X86_XSAVEOPT_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(0xD,1):EAX[0]: XSAVE that skips components unchanged since the
      last XRSTOR. Faster FP save on every switch. Fallback: XSAVE, then
      FXSAVE. require adds +xsaveopt. Needs X86_XSAVE.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_XSAVEOPT_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_XSAVEOPT_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_XSAVE_NEVER
    config X86_XSAVEOPT_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_XSAVE_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "XSAVES (compact, supervisor-state FP save)"
    default X86_XSAVES_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(0xD,1):EAX[3]: compacted XSAVE area with the init/modified
      optimisations and supervisor components (CET, PT). Smaller save area
      per task. Fallback: XSAVEOPT / XSAVE. require adds +xsaves. Needs
      X86_XSAVE.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_XSAVES_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_XSAVES_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
        depends on !X86_XSAVE_NEVER
    config X86_XSAVES_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
        depends on !X86_XSAVE_NEVER
endchoice

choice
    prompt "x2APIC (MSR-mapped LAPIC)"
    default X86_X2APIC_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[21]: LAPIC registers as MSRs, IPIs as one wrmsr to the
      ICR. Fallback: xAPIC MMIO at the IA32_APIC_BASE address. Cost: none.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_X2APIC_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_X2APIC_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_X2APIC_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "LAPIC TSC-deadline timer"
    default X86_TSC_DEADLINE_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[24]: Interrupts::set_timer_deadline writes
      IA32_TSC_DEADLINE in TSC ticks. Fallback: the LAPIC one-shot count
      calibrated against the TSC. Cost: none.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_TSC_DEADLINE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_TSC_DEADLINE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_TSC_DEADLINE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Invariant TSC"
    default X86_INVARIANT_TSC_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.0x80000007:EDX[8]: the TSC runs at a constant rate in every
      C/P-state, so a rate that CPUID leaf 15H or the hypervisor leaf
      states is the clock's. Without it (or under n) those stated rates are
      not taken: the TSC is measured against the HPET (X86_HPET), else the
      PIT, and CPUID 16H's nominal rate is the last resort. The [TIMER] boot
      line says which. Exported to ring 3 as HWCAP_X86_INVARIANT_TSC in
      the vDSO hwcap word (azos_libsys::vdso_hwcap) (a TSC delta is time).
      n: the measured path always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require:
      the boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_INVARIANT_TSC_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_INVARIANT_TSC_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_INVARIANT_TSC_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice
choice
    prompt "1 GiB pages (PDPE1GB)"
    default X86_GBPAGES_PROBE
    help
      Level-2 (PDPT) leaves after CPUID.80000001H:EDX[26]: one TLB entry
      per GiB for the kernel's direct map and the big fixed regions that
      ask for them. Cost: none at runtime. Fallback: Mmu::pte_make_leaf
      refuses a level-2 leaf and the caller maps 2 MiB leaves.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_GBPAGES_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_GBPAGES_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_GBPAGES_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "WAITPKG (UMONITOR/UMWAIT/TPAUSE)"
    default X86_WAITPKG_PROBE
    help
      The spin-wait hint of `SpinWait::wait_while` (crates/core/arch-api
      spin.rs): after SPIN_WAIT_RELAX_SPINS PAUSE polls the waiter arms
      UMONITOR on the lock word and sleeps in UMWAIT (C0.1 or C0.2,
      X86_UMWAIT_C02) until a store to the line, an interrupt, or the
      X86_UMWAIT_TSC_TICKS deadline. UMWAIT executes at any CPL (the U is
      "also user"; CR4.TSD only restricts CPL > 0), so ring 0 may use it;
      IA32_UMWAIT_CONTROL may cap the wait further. CPUID.(7,0):ECX[5].
      Fallback: the PAUSE loop. probe: one load of a boot-set flag and a
      branch per wait step (QEMU TCG does not implement WAITPKG, so the
      QEMU board always takes the fallback). n: PAUSE, no branch.
      require: inlined, no branch; a CPU without it is refused.

    config X86_WAITPKG_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_WAITPKG_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_WAITPKG_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

config X86_KERNEL_GLOBAL_PAGES
    bool "Global (G) kernel leaves"
    default y
    help
      Every kernel leaf carries G, so its TLB entry survives a CR3 write
      (CR4.PGE). Sound because the kernel lives in the upper half (the
      image at KERNEL_VA_OFFSET, PML4 slot 511; RAM in the direct map at
      DIRECT_MAP_BASE, slot 273 on) and every user root points at the
      kernel's own PDPTs there: a kernel leaf is the same word in every
      address space. The device windows in the low
      half are KERNEL_RW leaves too (G); a task's table borrows or copies
      them unchanged, and an unmap drops a G entry (invlpg, or the global
      flush). Cost of n: every address-space switch also drops the
      kernel's TLB entries, and the kernel re-walks its own text, data and
      stacks after each switch (dozens of misses per switch). Say n to
      measure that cost, or to rule G out while chasing a stale mapping.

# ── x86_64 trap entry and FP state ─────────────────────────────────────────
# kernel/src/entry/x86_64/ (cpu_init.rs, fp.rs, asm/trap_entry.S).

config X86_IST_STACK_SIZE_KB
    int "IST stack for #DF, NMI and #MC, each (KiB)"
    range 2 8
    default 4
    help
      The three exceptions that may arrive on a stack the kernel cannot
      trust (#DF after a fault while pushing, NMI and #MC because they
      ignore IF and can land inside syscall_entry before it leaves the user
      RSP) each get a known-good stack through the TSS IST slots.  They are
      carved from the TOP of each CPU's interrupt stack
      (INTERRUPT_STACK_SIZE_KB), so this costs no RAM of its own: it leaves
      INTERRUPT_STACK_SIZE_KB - 3 x this for device interrupt handlers, and
      the build refuses less than 4 KiB left.  4 KiB is Linux's
      EXCEPTION_STKSZ on every configuration without KASAN, and the same on
      every profile here: the handlers print and stop (#DF, #MC) or count
      (NMI), whatever the board.  Raise it only if a handler grows.

config X86_XSAVE_AREA_BYTES
    int "Per-task FP/SIMD save area (bytes, a multiple of 64)"
    range 576 8192
    default 2752 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    default 1024
    help
      The XSAVE image each task slot keeps for its ring-3 FP/SIMD state, and
      the copy a forked child inherits (Task::fork_regs).  The boot enables
      the largest XCR0 set that fits: AVX-512 needs about 2.7 KiB, AVX 832
      bytes, x87+SSE 576.  A CPU whose set does not fit is limited to the
      next smaller one, so user code there sees no AVX-512 (or no AVX).
      Cost: 2 x this x MAX_TASKS of .bss (edge, 64 tasks: 128 KiB at the
      1 KiB default; fleet, 4096 tasks at 2752: 22 MiB); time: one
      XSAVEOPT/XRSTOR pair per context switch that changes the ring-3 owner,
      proportional to the components in use, not to this size.  fleet
      defaults to AVX-512 (server parts have it); edge and embedded to AVX.

choice
    prompt "Split-lock detection (IA32_CORE_CAPABILITIES, MSR_TEST_CTRL)"
    default X86_SPLIT_LOCK_PROBE
    help
      MSR_TEST_CTRL bit 29: a locked atomic that crosses a cache line raises
      #AC instead of taking a bus lock that stalls every other core for
      microseconds (jitter a real-time task on another core cannot bound).
      Present when CPUID.(7,0):EDX[30] enumerates IA32_CORE_CAPABILITIES and
      its bit 5 is set (Intel Tremont / Ice Lake and later); every MSR access
      is behind that probe, so a CPU or an emulator without it (QEMU TCG) is
      never touched. A split lock in ring 3 kills the task with a decision
      record (`split-lock`, in the flight recorder); in the kernel see
      X86_SPLIT_LOCK_KERNEL_PANIC. The MSR is per core: every CPU writes it.
      Cost: none until a split lock happens.
      n: never enabled, the bus lock is taken. probe: the CPUID/MSR probe
      picks. require: the boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_SPLIT_LOCK_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_SPLIT_LOCK_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_SPLIT_LOCK_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Performance counters (Intel architectural PMU, AMD core counters)"
    default X86_PMU_PROBE
    help
      The source of `Pmu` on x86_64, read with `rdpmc` (ring 0 may always).
      Intel: CPUID leaf 0xA version 2 or later gives fixed counter 0
      (INST_RETIRED.ANY, `Pmu::instructions`) and 1 (CPU_CLK_UNHALTED,
      `Pmu::cycles`), programmed through IA32_FIXED_CTR_CTRL and
      IA32_PERF_GLOBAL_CTRL, and general counter 0 for `Pmu::event`
      (IA32_PERFEVTSEL0). AMD: PerfCtrExtCore (CPUID 0x80000001 ECX[23], six
      counters at MSR 0xC0010200; PerfMonV2 CPUID 0x80000022 gives their
      number and PerfCntrGlobalCtl) or, on bare metal only, the four legacy
      counters; counter 0 counts event 0x76 (cycles not halted), 1 event
      0xC0 (retired instructions), 2 serves `Pmu::event`. A counter that
      does not move after it is enabled (a hypervisor that ignores the MSR
      write) is not used. Every PMU MSR is behind the CPUID probe: a CPU or
      an emulator without it (QEMU TCG) is never touched.
      Fallback: `Pmu::cycles` is the time-stamp counter (the rate is
      measured at boot) and `Pmu::instructions` is 0. n: the same.
      require: a CPU without the cycle and instruction counters is refused.

    config X86_PMU_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_PMU_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_PMU_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AMD SVM hypervisor primitives (EFER.SVME, VMRUN, NPT)"
    default X86_SVM_PROBE
    help
      The hypervisor's AMD half (arch-x86_64 ext/hv): EFER.SVME per CPU, the
      VMCB, nested page tables, VMRUN and the #VMEXIT decode. Present when
      CPUID.0x80000001:ECX[2] says so and VM_CR.SVMDIS is clear (firmware
      can lock SVM off: then the CPU reports no hypervisor, with a record,
      and the boot goes on). QEMU TCG implements SVM (`-cpu max,+svm`).
      Cost: none until a guest exists; the probe is one CPUID leaf and one
      MSR read.
      n: never touched. probe: the CPUID/MSR probe picks. require: the boot
      refuses a CPU without usable SVM.

    config X86_SVM_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_SVM_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_SVM_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Intel VMX hypervisor (VMX, EPT, VMLAUNCH)"
    default X86_VMX_PROBE
    help
      The hypervisor's Intel half (arch-x86_64 ext/hv: vmx, ept, vmx_hw): the
      same guest entry as the AMD half over a VMCS and extended page tables.
      VMX is present when CPUID.1:ECX[5] says so, IA32_FEATURE_CONTROL allows
      VMXON outside SMX (firmware can lock it off: then the CPU reports no
      hypervisor, with a record, and the boot goes on) and the capability
      MSRs allow a 4-level write-back EPT walk, INVEPT and unrestricted guest
      (the AzOS guest starts with paging off and its other CPUs in real
      mode). SVM stays primary: an AMD CPU never picks VMX. QEMU's TCG does
      not implement VMX, so the instruction layer is not exercised by the
      host tests; the encoders and the translation are host-tested against
      the SDM's numbers.
      Cost: the probe is one CPUID leaf and a few MSR reads; a CPU enters
      VMX operation (CR4.VMXE, VMXON) only when it first runs a guest.
      n: never touched. probe: the CPUID/MSR probe picks. require: the boot
      refuses a CPU without usable VMX.

    config X86_VMX_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_VMX_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_VMX_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

config X86_SPLIT_LOCK_KERNEL_PANIC
    bool "x86_64: a split lock in the kernel panics (development) instead of warning"
    default y if KTEST
    default n
    help
      With X86_SPLIT_LOCK in use, a split-locked atomic raising #AC in ring 0
      is a kernel bug. y: panic with the instruction pointer (development
      and test kernels, where the bug should stop the run). n: warn once per
      rate-limit window and clear MSR_TEST_CTRL bit 29 on that CPU so the
      access retries (a #AC is a fault: returning re-executes it), after
      which that CPU takes bus locks again. Unused without X86_SPLIT_LOCK.

config X86_SYSRET
    bool "Return from system calls with sysretq"
    default y
    help
      y: a system call returns through sysretq when the frame still holds
      what syscall saved (RIP = RCX, RFLAGS = R11), RIP is canonical and
      RFLAGS has neither TF nor RF; anything else takes iretq.  That is
      Linux's rule: a non-canonical RIP makes sysretq fault in ring 0 on
      Intel with the user RSP already loaded (CVE-2012-0217).
      n: always iretq, which is microcoded and slower than sysretq on every
      system call (not measured here yet); the simplest return path, for
      bring-up or bisecting.

endif # ARCH_X86_64
